diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce a routing outage between the CS lab (192.168.2.0/24) and the Server Room (1.0.0.0/8). From multiple CS lab hosts (cs_lab1–cs_lab4), ICMP to the server segment succeeds: each host cleanly pings 1.0.0.5 with 0% loss and ~1–2 ms RTT. Routing is also correct across the campus routers. On router0 (CS edge), the RIP-learned routes include 1.0.0.0/8 via 10.10.0.2 on eth2 (wan_r0_r1), and 20.0.0.0/8/128.168.0.0/16 via the same next hop. Router1 and router2 reciprocally have the expected RIP routes (router1 sees 1.0.0.0/8 via 20.20.0.2 on eth3; router2 has 192.168.2.0/24 via 20.20.0.1), with recent timers, confirming end-to-end advertisements are active. CS hosts have the correct default gateway (192.168.2.1 on router0 eth1).

Mechanism: Because RIP is advertising 1.0.0.0/8 from router2 across router1 to router0, the data path is intact; successful pings from 192.168.2.x to 1.0.0.5 demonstrate forwarding and return routing both work. Localization: No fault observed on router0–router2 or inter-router links (10.10.0.0/8 and 20.20.0.0/8).

Root cause: No routing issue is present at this time; the earlier symptom was likely transient or application-layer.

Corrective fix: No router change needed. If file shares are still inaccessible, investigate the servers themselves: on 1.0.0.5 there are no application listeners (netstat shows only 127.0.0.11), which would explain share/“server vanished” symptoms despite healthy network reachability. Start the required services on the server if needed. === END ===